    "Stem": "CFO BRIEFING MEMO — Apex Industrial Supply\n\nTO: Marcus Chen, CFO\nFROM: Priya Kapoor, Director of FP&A\nRE: Budget Methodology Assessment — FY2026 Performance and FY2027 Recommendation\n\nApex distributes industrial components to 900+ manufacturing customers across the Midwest. Our current budgeting approach is an annual static budget finalized in November, based on a single-point demand forecast. Over the past 8 quarters, demand volatility — measured by the coefficient of variation of quarterly revenue — increased from 8% (FY2022-FY2024 average) to 22% (FY2025-FY2026). The impact on budget reliability has been severe:\n\n| Metric | Annual Static Budget (Current) | Rolling 12-Month Forecast (Proposed) |\n|--------|-------------------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| 8-Quarter MAPE vs. Actual Revenue | 14.2% | 8.7% |\n| Q3 FY2026 Operating Income Miss | ($340,000) below budget | ($125,000) estimated |\n| Update Frequency | Annually (November) | Quarterly (every 3 months) |\n| Implementation Cost | $0 (existing) | $45,000 per quarter ($180,000/year) |\n| Forecast Horizon | Deteriorates through year | Constant 12 months forward |\n\nOperating income fell 34% below the FY2026 static budget in Q3 because the November 2025 demand forecast was based on 8% CV assumptions that became obsolete by March 2026 when two large customers shifted to just-in-time ordering. The rolling forecast model would have surfaced this demand pattern change by Q1 2026, allowing Apex to adjust purchasing and staffing. However, the $180,000 annual implementation cost represents 0.9% of Apex's $20 million operating budget. The Board has asked whether the forecast accuracy improvement justifies this cost. Which recommendation should the CFO present?",
    "Choices": {
      "A": "Retain the annual static budget because it costs nothing to operate and $180,000 per year will not be recovered through modest forecast accuracy improvement alone.",
      "B": "Adopt the rolling 12-month forecast for FY2027. A 5.5 percentage-point MAPE improvement translates to approximately $280,000-$350,000 in avoided cost overruns from better purchasing and staffing decisions, exceeding the $180,000 annual cost. The constant 12-month horizon eliminates the deteriorating-forecast problem inherent in annual static budgets under high demand volatility.",
      "C": "Split the difference: keep the annual budget for cost control and performance evaluation but add a quarterly rolling forecast as a supplementary planning tool without changing the budget authorization process.",
      "D": "Abandon formal budgets entirely. Replace both the annual budget and rolling forecast with a continuous planning model where division managers receive real-time spending authority based on trailing 90-day revenue, eliminating the forecasting problem altogether."
    },
    "CorrectChoice": "B",
    "ExplanationCorrect": "The quantitative evidence and cost-benefit analysis both support adopting the rolling 12-month forecast (Choice B). The core problem is that Apex's annual static budget is built on a single-point forecast that becomes increasingly unreliable as the fiscal year progresses — the 14.2% MAPE reflects forecasts made 3-11 months ago, weighted toward periods when assumptions are most stale. Under demand volatility of 22% CV (nearly triple the historical 8%), a forecast prepared in November cannot reasonably predict Q3 demand conditions. The rolling forecast corrects this structurally: each quarter, the completed period drops off and a new 12th-month period is added, maintaining a constant forward-looking horizon built on the most current demand data. The cost-benefit analysis is favorable: at Apex's $20 million operating budget, a 5.5 percentage-point MAPE improvement implies avoided budget misses of approximately $280,000-$350,000 — conservative given that Q3 FY2026 alone produced a $340,000 operating income miss under the static budget. The $180,000 annual cost is recovered if the rolling forecast prevents even one quarter of the magnitude Apex experienced in Q3 FY2026. From a CMA Part 1 perspective, rolling budgets (continuous budgets) are particularly appropriate when: (a) demand or cost volatility is high, (b) forecast accuracy deteriorates significantly over the budget period, and (c) the cost of implementation is exceeded by the value of improved planning accuracy. All three conditions are present at Apex. A common exam trap is treating rolling forecasts as substitutes for annual budgets — they serve different purposes. The annual budget provides authorization and performance benchmarks; the rolling forecast provides updated planning guidance. In practice, organizations often maintain both, but the question asks which methodology Apex should adopt for its primary budgeting process. Under Apex's specific circumstances, the rolling forecast as the primary planning tool is the economically justified recommendation.",
