    "ExplanationCorrect": "The two best ZBB pilot candidates are IT Services (B) and Facilities Management (D). The selection logic rests on two ZBB principles. First, ZBB provides the greatest benefit in departments with high discretionary spending — spending not automatically determined by prior-year levels or contractual obligations. IT Services at 74% discretionary and Facilities Management at 45% discretionary have substantial portions of their budgets that are not locked into mandatory spending, meaning a zero-base review can identify genuine opportunities to eliminate, reduce, or restructure activities. Second, departments with the highest cost growth rates (+21.8% for IT, +18.4% for Facilities) are the most likely to have accumulated spending under incremental budgeting that would not survive a zero-base justification. The combination of high growth and high discretionary spending creates the strongest case for ZBB: these departments have both the opportunity (flexible spending) and the indication (rapid growth) that current spending levels exceed necessary spending levels. Marketing (A) has high discretionary spending (62%) but very low cost growth (4.1%), suggesting its current spending trajectory is already controlled. Legal (E) has low discretionary spending (19%) because most legal costs are driven by litigation, compliance requirements, and contractual obligations — ZBB would add process cost without substantial savings opportunity. Human Resources (C) has moderate cost growth (5.6%) and low discretionary spending (31%), making it a poor ZBB candidate. Absolute budget size is not the selection driver — ZBB targets the spending that is most likely unjustified, which correlates with high growth rates in discretionary categories rather than large budgets in categories where spending is largely non-discretionary.",
