    "Stem": "POST-IMPLEMENTATION REVIEW — Harborview Medical Center\n\nTO: Audit Committee\nFROM: Internal Audit Department\nRE: ZBB Pilot — One-Year Assessment and Expansion Recommendation\n\nHarborview implemented zero-based budgeting (ZBB) in three support departments for FY2026: Radiology Administration, Facilities Management, and Human Resources. The remaining five support departments continued under incremental budgeting. The pilot's goal was to determine whether ZBB should be expanded enterprise-wide for FY2027. After one year, Internal Audit has compiled the following results:\n\nDepartment | Budget Method | FY2025 Cost (Pre-ZBB) | FY2026 Cost | Cost Change | Manager Hours Spent on Budget Prep | % of Budget Deemed Discretionary\nRadiology Admin | ZBB | $2,840,000 | $2,510,000 | -11.6% | 420 hours | 58%\nFacilities Mgmt | ZBB | $3,620,000 | $2,980,000 | -17.7% | 510 hours | 64%\nHuman Resources | ZBB | $1,940,000 | $1,810,000 | -6.7% | 380 hours | 31%\nIT Services | Incremental | $4,120,000 | $4,390,000 | +6.6% | 180 hours | 72%\nFinance | Incremental | $2,260,000 | $2,420,000 | +7.1% | 160 hours | 28%\nMarketing | Incremental | $3,180,000 | $3,370,000 | +6.0% | 200 hours | 63%\n\nKey observations: Radiology and Facilities achieved double-digit cost reductions. HR showed modest improvement. The three ZBB departments averaged 437 hours of manager time on budget preparation vs. 180 hours for incremental departments. Two department heads (Radiology, Facilities) rated the process as 'worth the effort'; HR rated it 'burdensome relative to savings.' IT Services and Marketing, which remained on incremental budgeting, grew costs 6-7% despite having the highest discretionary spend percentages. The CEO must decide whether to expand ZBB enterprise-wide for FY2027. Based on the pilot evidence, which recommendation should Internal Audit present to the Audit Committee?",
